ws a*k..(a+1)k-1, columns 0..a*k-1. Similarly, the right regions across all block columns form a rectangle of rows a*k..(a+1)k-1, columns (a+1)*k .. n-1 (since each block contributes k-1-a columns). So each block row a yields two rectangles: left block and right block. So total horizontal rectangles = 2k.\n\nNow we also need to cover the squares that are in the same column as the holes but not in the same block row? Actually the holes are at positions (a*k + b, b*k + a). The left and right rectangles cover all squares in block row a except the columns that contain the holes? Wait, the left rectangle covers columns 0..a*k-1, which includes all columns that are in block columns less than a. The right rectangle covers columns (a+1)*k .. n-1, which includes all columns in block columns greater than a. The columns in block column a (i.e., columns a*k .. (a+1)k-1) are not covered by these two rectangles, because those columns are the block column a. Within block column a, there are k blocks (block rows b). In block column a, the holes are at positions (b*k + a, a*k + b). So in block column a, each row block b has a hole at column a*k + b (i.e., column offset b within block column a). So within block column a, the squares not covered by the horizontal rectangles are those in rows a*k..(a+1)k-1? Actually the horizontal rectangles only cover rows a*k..(a+1)k-1 (block row a). They do not cover any squares outside block row a. So the squares in block column a but rows outside block row a are not covered yet. Those squares can be covered by vertical rectangles similarly: For each block column a, we can cover the squares above block row a and below block row a using vertical rectangles. Let's examine.\n\nConsider block column a. The rows are partitioned into k blocks of height k: block rows b = 0..k-1. In block column a, the hole in block row b is at row index b*k + a (i.e., row offset a within block row b). So within block column a, the region above the hole in block row b consists of a rows (0..a-1) within that block row b, and region below consists of k-1-a rows (a+1..k-1). So across all block rows b, the region above the holes in each block row b forms a vertical rectangle: columns a*k..(a+1)k-1, rows 0..a*k-1 (since each block row contributes a rows). Similarly, region below holes across all block rows b forms a vertical rectangle: columns a*k..(a+1)k-1, rows (a+1)*k .. n-1. So each block column a yields two vertical rectangles: top rectangle and bottom rectangle. So total vertical rectangles = 2k.\n\nNow we have covered all squares except the squares that are in the intersection of block rows and block columns? Let's check: The horizontal rectangles covered all squares in block rows a, columns not in block column a (i.e., columns left or right of block column a). The vertical rectangles cover all squares in block columns a, rows not in block row a (i.e., rows above or below block row a). The only squares not yet covered are those that lie in block rows a and block columns a simultaneously, i.e., the k x k blocks on the diagonal (block row a, block column a). In each such diagonal block, there is exactly one hole at position (a*k + a, a*k + a) (since b = a). The rest of the diagonal block (size k x k minus one cell) is not covered by the horizontal or vertical rectangles because those rectangles avoid the diagonal blocks entirely (horizontal rectangles skip block column a, vertical rectangles skip block row a). So we need to cover the diagonal blocks.\n\nThus we have k diagonal blocks each of size k x k with one hole. We need to cover each diagonal block with some rectangles. Since each diagonal block is isolated, we can treat each separately. For each diagonal block, we need to cover k^2 - 1 squares. The minimal number of rectangles needed to cover a k x k block with one missing cell is k + (k - 1) = 2k - 1? Let's examine.\n\nWe can cover a k x k block with one missing cell using k rectangles: cover each row except the row containing