ve formula: f(n) = n + 2 sqrt(n) - 3.\n\n- Provide construction achieving that many tiles: Partition rows and columns into k blocks of size k. Place holes at positions (a*k + b, b*k + a) for 0 ≤ a,b < k. This yields exactly one hole per row and column, arranged in a \"grid transversal\". Then cover the complement with:\n\n   * For each block row a, use two horizontal rectangles: one covering the k rows a*k..(a+1)k-1 and the columns 0..a*k-1 (left part), and another covering the same rows and columns (a+1)k..n-1 (right part). This gives 2k horizontal rectangles.\n\n   * For each block column a, use two vertical rectangles: one covering the columns a*k..(a+1)k-1 and rows 0..a*k-1 (top part), and another covering the same columns and rows (a+1)k..n-1 (bottom part). This gives another 2k vertical rectangles.\n\n   * The intersection of the horizontal and vertical rectangles leaves exactly the k diagonal k×k blocks, each missing one cell at offset a. Each such diagonal block can be covered with three rectangles when a = 0 or a = k-1, and with four rectangles otherwise. Summing over a = 0,…,k-1 yields total rectangles for diagonal blocks = 4k - 2.\n\n   * However, we can merge the two horizontal rectangles of block row a with the two vertical rectangles of block column a to reduce the count: Instead of using all four rectangles per block row/column, we keep only the 2k horizontal rectangles and the 2k vertical rectangles, but we can discard the vertical rectangles for the first and last block columns (a = 0 and a = k-1) because their top/bottom parts are already covered by the horizontal rectangles. Consequently the total number of rectangles reduces to n + 2k - 3.\n\nBut this reasoning is messy. Let's find a simpler construction that yields exactly n + 2k - 3 rectangles.\n\nSimpler construction: Use the same hole placement as above. Then cover the complement as follows:\n\n- For each row i, except the rows i where i mod k = k-1 (i.e., the last row of each block), we cove