{(i,i):1\\le i\\le n\\}$.\n\n#### 2.  Geometry of admissible rectangles\n\nLet $R=I\\times J$ be a rectangle that does **not** contain any diagonal square.  If $I=[a,b]$ and $J=[c,d]$ (with $a\\le b$, $c\\le d$) then for every $i\\in I$ we must have $i\\notin J$.  Consequently either $d<i$ for all $i\\in I$ or $c>i$ for all $i\\in I$.  In the first case $J\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots,a-1\\}$; we call $R$ **left\u2011anchored**.  In the second case $J\\subseteq\\{b+1,\\dots,n\\}$; we call $R$ **right\u2011anchored**.  Thus every admissible rectangle is of one of the two forms\n\\[\\text{left\u2011anchored: }[s,n]\\times[1,c]\\quad(c<s),\\qquad\\text{right\u2011anchored: }[1,s]\\times[d,n]\\quad(s<d).\\]\nThe lower triangular region\n\\[L=\\{(i,j):i>j\\}\\]\ncan be covered only by left\u2011anchored rectangles, and the upper triangular region\n\\[U=\\{(i,j):i<j\\}\\]\nonly by right\u2011anchored rectangles.\n\n#### 3.  A lower bound for the lower triangular part\n\nConsider any family $\\mathcal{L}$ of left\u2011anchored rectangles that covers $L$.  Write each rectangle $R\\in\\mathcal{L}$ as $R=[s_R,n]\\times[1,c_R]$ with $c_R<s_R$.  For $i\\in\\{1,\\dots,n\\}$ let\n\\[f(i)=\\max\\{c_R\\mid s_R\\le i\\},\\]\nwith the convention $f(i)=0$ if the set is empty.  Because $\\mathcal{L}$ covers $L$, for each row $i$ the columns $1,\\dots,i-1$ must be covered, hence\n\\[f(i)\\ge i-1\\qquad\\text{for all }i.\\tag{1}\\]\nThe function $f$ is non\u2011decreasing, and each rectangle $R$ can increase $f$ only at its starting row $s_R$, because $c_R$ does not affect $f(i)$ for $i<s_R$.  Therefore the number of distinct values taken by $f$ is at most $|\\mathcal{L}|$.\n\nNow observe that the inequality (1) forces $f$ to reach the value $n-1$.  Starting from $f(1)=0$, each increase can at most double the current value: if $f$ jumps from $c$ to $c'$ at row $s$, then $c'=c_R\\le s-1$, and the jump occurs only when $s\\le c'+1$.  Consequently $c'\\le 2c+1$.  By induction, after $k$ jumps we have $f\\le 2^{k}-1$.  To achieve $f\\ge n-1$ we need $2^{k}-1\\ge n-1$, i.e. $2^{k}\\ge n$.  Hence $k\\ge\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil$.\nSince $k\\le|\\mathcal{L}|$, any covering of $L$ uses at least $\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil$ left\u2011anchored rectangles.\n\nThe same argument applied to the upper triangular part $U$ (by reflecting the board) shows that any covering of $U$ needs at least $\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil$ right\u2011anchored rectangles.\n\nThus **any** tiling of the whole board requires at least\n\\[2\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil\\]\nrectangles.\n\n#### 4.  A matching construction\n\nWe now exhibit a covering that uses exactly $2\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil$ rectangles.\n\n**Covering $L$ (left\u2011anchored rectangles).**\nSet $n_0=n$.  For $t=1,2,\\dots$ define\n\\[c_t=\\left\\lfloor\\frac{n_{t-1}}{2}\\right\\rfloor,\\qquad R_t^{L}=\\{(i,j):c_t+1\\le i\\le n_{t-1},\\;1\\le j\\le c_t\\}.\n\\]\nEach $R_t^{L}$ is left\u2011anchored because $j\\le c_t<c_t+1\\le i$.  The rows used by $R_t^{L}$ are a suffix of the current triangle, and the columns are a prefix; therefore the rectangles $R_1^{L},R_2^{L},\\dots$ are pairwise disjoint.  After removing $R_t^{L}$ the uncovered part of $L$ is exactly the lower triangle of size $c_t$, so we replace $n_{t-1}$ by $c_t$ and repeat.  The process stops when $c_t=0$, i.e. after $k$ steps where $k$ is the smallest integer with $2^{k}\\ge n$.  Hence $k=\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil$, and the family $\\{R_t^{L}\\}_{t=1}^{k}$ covers $L$.\n\n**Covering $U$ (right\u2011anchored rectangles).**\nApply the symmetric construction: for the same sequence $c_t$, define\n\\[R_t^{U}=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le c_t,\\;c_t+1\\le j\\le n_{t-1}\\}.\n\\]\nEach $R_t^{U}$ is right\u2011anchored because $i\\le c_t< c_t+1\\le j$, the rectangles are disjoint, and together they cover $U$.\n\nThe $2k$ rectangles $R_t^{L},R_t^{U}$ are disjoint, avoid all diagonal squares, and together cover every off\u2011diagonal square.  Consequently a tiling with $2\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil$ tiles exists.\n\n#### 5.  Evaluation for $n=2025$\n\nSince $2^{10}=1024<2025\\le 2^{11}=2048$, we have $\\lceil\\log_2 2025\\rceil=11$.  The construction above yields $2\\times11=22$ rectangles, and the lower\u2011bound argument shows that $21$ rectangles are impossible.  Therefore the minimum number of tiles required is **22**.\n\n### Final Answer ###\nThe minimum number of rectangular tiles Matilda must place is 22.\n",
    "final_answer": 22
}
