6. For i in [254,506], f(i) = 253, etc. So f(i) is a step function that jumps at each start row s_t. The jumps are large: from 0 to 1012 at i = 1013, then from 506 at i = 507, etc. So the earlier bound that each jump can increase f by at most +1 is false because we incorrectly assumed that f(i-1) >= i-2 must hold for all i. But after the final covering, f(i) must satisfy f(i) >= i-1 for all i. However, the function f(i) defined as max c_R for s_R <= i may not be monotonic? It is monotonic non-decreasing because as i increases, more rectangles become eligible, so max can only increase. But does it satisfy f(i) >= i-1 for all i? Let's test for i = 1012: f(1012) = max c_R for s_R <= 1012. The rectangles with start rows <= 1012 are those with start rows s_2 = 507, s_3 = 254, ..., s_k. The max c_R among those is c_2 = 506. So f(1012) = 506. But we need f(1012) >= 1011 (since i-1 = 1011). This is not true. So the condition f(i) >= i-1 does not hold for this construction. Wait, but we defined f(i) as max c_R for s_R <= i. However, the union of columns covered in row i is not necessarily [1, f(i)], because each rectangle covers columns [1, c_R] for rows from its start row to n. But for row i = 1012, which rectangles cover row i? The rectangles that have start row s_R <= i (i.e., start row <= 1012) cover row i because they cover rows from s_R to n. So all rectangles with start row <= 1012 cover row i. The columns covered in row i are the union of [1, c_R] for all such rectangles. Since each rectangle covers columns from 1 to c_R, the union is indeed [1, max c_R] = [1, f(i)]. So row i = 1012 is covered only up to column 506, not up to column 1011. But row 1012 must have all columns up to 1011 covered because we need to cover all lower triangle points (i>j). So the construction fails to cover the lower triangle? Let's examine the construction more carefully. The construction is supposed to cover the lower triangle L, which includes all points (i,j) with i>j. The construction uses rectangles R_t^L that cover rows from c_t+1 to n_{t-1} and columns 1..c_t. Let's see if the union of these rectangles indeed covers all points (i,j) with i>j.\n\nTake n = 2025. c_1 = floor(2025/2) = 1012. R_1^L covers rows 1013..2025, columns 1..1012. So it covers all points (i,j) with i >= 1013 and j <= 1012. In particular, for any i >= 1013, all j < i are covered because j <= i-1, but j may be > 1012 if i is large. For i = 1500, j can be up to 1499, but R_1^L only covers j up to 1012. So points (1500, 1200) are not covered by R_1^L. However, later rectangles may cover those points. Let's examine R_2^L: c_2 = floor(1012/2) = 506. R_2^L covers rows 507..1012, columns 1..506. So it covers points with i in [507,1012] and j <= 506. So for i = 1500, row 1500 is not covered by R_2^L because its rows are only up to 1012. So points (1500, 1200) remain uncovered after R_1 and R_2. Next rectangle R_3^L: c_3 = floor(506/2) = 253. R_3^L covers rows 254..506, columns 1..253. So still not covering row 1500. So the construction only covers rows up to n_{t-1} = the current size (starting from n_0 = 2025, then n_1 = 1012, n_2 = 506, etc.). So after k steps, the largest row covered by any rectangle is n_0 = 2025 (by R_1). The next rectangles cover rows up to n_1 = 1012, then n_2 = 506, etc. So the union of all rectangles covers rows in intervals: [c_1+1, n_0] = [1013,2025] (R_1), [c_2+1, n_1] = [507,1012] (R_2), [c_3+1, n_2] = [254,506] (R_3), etc., down to [c_k+1, n_{k-1}] = [2, something]. So the union of rows covered is the whole range [2,2025] (since the intervals are nested and cover all rows >= 2). Indeed, the intervals partition the rows: each row i belongs to exactly one rectangle: find the smallest t such that i > c_t (i.e., i >= c_t+1) and i <= n_{t-1}. Since c_t = floor(n_{t-1}/2), the intervals are [floor(n_{t-1}/2)+1, n_{t-1}]. So they partition the rows: each row i belongs to the interval where i > floor(n_{t-1}/2) and i <= n_{t-1}. So each row is covered by exactly one rectangle. For each such row i, the rectangle covering it has columns 1..c_t, where c_t = floor(n_{t-1}/2). So for row i in that interval, the rectangle covers columns up to c_t, which is roughly half of the maximum column index needed for that row (i-1). For row i near the top of the interval (i close to n_{t-1}), we need to cover columns up to i-1, which may be larger than c_t. For example, row i = n_{t-1} (the top of the interval) needs to cover columns up to n_{t-1} - 1. But the rectangle covering that row only covers columns up to c_t = floor(n_{t-1}/2). So many columns remain uncovered for that row. However, those columns may be covered by rectangles that cover higher rows (i.e., rectangles with larger start rows). But those rectangles cover rows from a larger start row to n, i.e., they cover rows with i >= start row. For row i = n_{t-1}, the only rectangle covering it is the one with start row <= i, i.e., any rectangle with start row <= n_{t-1}. The rectangles with start row > n_{t-1} (i.e., those covering rows above n_{t-1}) do not exist because n_{t-1} is the maximum row index. So the only rectangle covering row i = n_{t-1} is the rectangle that has start row <= n_{t-1} and includes row i. Which rectangle includes row i? The rectangle R_t^L covers rows from c_t+1 to n_{t-1}, so it includes row i = n_{t-1}. So row i = n_{t-1} is covered only by rectangle R_t^L, which covers columns 1..c_t. So columns beyond c_t are not covered. Therefore, the construction fails to cover the lower triangle completely. Let's test with a concrete small case to see if the construction works.\n\nTake n = 5. Then c_1 = floor(5/2) = 2. R_1^L = rows 3..5, columns 1..2. So covers points: (3,1),(3,2),(4,1),(4,2),(5,1),(5,2). The lower triangle L for n=5 includes points: (2,1), (3,1),(3,2), (4,1),(4,2),(4,3), (5,1),(5,2),(5,3),(5,4). After R_1, we have covered (3,1),(3,2),(4,1),(4,2),(5,1),(5,2). Uncovered points: (2,1),(4,3),(5,3),(5,4). Next, n_1 = c_1 = 2. c_2 = floor(2/2) = 1