ne more generally. For row i, the maximum column we can cover is i-1 (since column i is diagonal). So f(i) <= i-1. So the function f(i) is bounded by i-1. So the jump from c to c' cannot exceed the difference between i-1 and c. But the doubling argument seems to be a known argument: each rectangle can at most double the covered columns because of the condition c_R < s_R. Let's derive a bound: Suppose we have f(i-1) = c. For a rectangle that starts at row s = i, we have c_R < s = i. So c_R <= i-1. Since f(i-1) = c, we have c <= i-2? Actually, f(i-1) = c <= (i-1)-1 = i-2, because row i-1 cannot cover column i-1 (diagonal). So c <= i-2. So c_R <= i-1 = c+? Actually, c_R <= i-1 = (c+2?) Let's compute: Since c <= i-2, i-1 <= c+1? Actually, i-2 >= c, so i-1 >= c+1. So c_R <= i-1 <= c+? Wait, i-1 = (i-2)+1 >= c+1. So c_R <= i-1, which could be as large as c+? If c = i-2, then i-1 = c+1. So c_R <= c+1. So the jump from c to c_R can be at most +1. So the doubling argument is too generous; the actual bound is c' <= c+1. But the solution's argument that c' <= 2c+1 is weaker (allows bigger jumps). So the bound they derive (k >= ceil(log2 n)) is still valid because they allow larger jumps, so they may overestimate the necessary number of rectangles? Actually, they claim each jump can at most double the current value: c' <= 2c+1. If the actual bound is c' <= c+1, then the number of jumps needed to reach n-1 is at least n-1 (i.e., linear), not logarithmic. But that seems too strong; we need to examine carefully.\n\nLet's examine the function f(i) defined as max c_R for s_R <= i. Actually, they define f(i) = max{c_R | s_R <= i}. This is the maximum column index among rectangles whose start row is <= i. Since rectangles are left-anchored [s_R,n] x [1,c_R], they cover rows from s_R to n. So for any row i >= s_R, the rectangle covers columns up to c_R. So f(i) is the maximum column covered among all rectangles that start at or before row i. So f(i) is monotone non-decreasing. For each