m s_R to n, columns from 1 to c_R. So for any row i >= s_R, the rectangle covers columns up to c_R. So f(i) is the maximum column covered among all rectangles that start at or before i. So for i = s_R - 1, the rectangle does not cover row i, so f(s_R - 1) does not consider c_R. So f(s_R - 1) is determined by rectangles with start row <= s_R - 1. So the condition that f(s_R - 1) >= s_R - 2 (since row s_R - 1 must have columns up to s_R - 2 covered) holds. So c >= s_R - 2. So s_R <= c + 2. So indeed s_R is at most c+2. So the next rectangle cannot start much later than c+2. So the jump from c to c_R is at most c_R <= s_R - 1 <= (c+2) - 1 = c+1. So the increase is at most +1. So each rectangle can increase f by at most 1. So the number of rectangles needed to increase f from 0 to n-1 is at least n-1. That would imply a lower bound of n-1 rectangles for covering L, which is far larger than the construction using O(log n). Something is off.\n\nLet's examine the definitions again. The rectangle R = [s_R,n] x [1,c_R] is left-anchored. But is it true that any left-anchored rectangle must be of this form? Actually, a left-anchored rectangle could be any rectangle where J is left of I. But they restrict to rectangles that extend to the bottom and left edges. However, the covering of L using O(log n) rectangles uses rectangles that are indeed of that form: they are corner rectangles that cover a suffix of rows and prefix of columns. The construction uses R_t^L = { (i,j): c_t+1 <= i <= n_{t-1}, 1 <= j <= c_t }. This is indeed of the form [s,n] x [1,c] where s = c_t+1, c = c_t. So the construction uses left-anchored rectangles of that specific form.\n\nNow, the lower bound argument is trying to show that any covering of L using left-anchored rectangles of that specific form requires at least ceil(log2 n) rectangles. That seems plausible: The function f(i) defined as max c_R for s_R <= i, with each rectangle's c_R < s_R. The condition f(i) >= i-1 for all i. The bound they derive is that each rectangle can increase f by at most doubling (c' <= 2c+1). However, we derived a stronger bound c' <= c+1. Let's check if we made a mistake.\n\nLet's re-evaluate the condition f(i) >= i-1. For row i, we need to cover columns 1..i-1. However, the rectangle covering row i may not necessarily cover all columns up to i-1; it could be that multiple rectangles together cover the columns up to i-1. The function f(i) is defined as the maximum column index covered by any rectangle that starts at or before i. But covering columns up to i-1 may require multiple rectangles covering different column intervals. The function f(i) only captures the maximum column covered by any single rectangle, not the union of columns covered by multiple rectangles. So the condition f(i) >= i-1 is not necessary; we only need that the union of columns covered by all rectangles that intersect row i covers [1,i-1]. But f(i) being the maximum column covered by any rectangle that starts at or before i is insufficient to guarantee coverage of all columns up to i-1. For example, suppose we have two rectangles: one covering columns 1..5 for rows 10..n, another covering columns 6..10 for rows 10..n. Then for row i=10, the union covers columns 1..10, but f(10) = max{c_R} = 10. So f(10) >= 9, okay. But suppose we have rectangles covering columns 1..3 and 4..6 for row i=10. Then f(10) = 6, which is >=9? No, it's not. But the union covers columns 1..6, not up to 9. So coverage fails. But if we have rectangles covering columns 1..3 and 4..9, then f(10) = 9, which is >=9. So f(i) >= i-1 is necessary but not sufficient? Actually, we need union of columns to cover up to i-1. The maximum column covered by any rectangle that starts at or before i must be at least i-1, because otherwise there would be some column > max that is uncovered. However, consider row i=10: Suppose we have rectangles covering columns 1..5 (max c_R =5) and columns 6..9 (max c_R =9). The max is 9, which is >=9, so condition holds.