lower triangle includes points (i,j) with i>j. For row 1012, we need to cover columns 1..1011. The construction hasn't covered row 1012 yet because the first rectangle covers rows 1013..2025 only. So row 1012 is uncovered after the first rectangle. But the construction will later cover row 1012 in later rectangles: R_2 covers rows 507..1012, columns 1..506. That covers columns 1..506 for row 1012, but not columns 507..1011. However, after we apply R_3, etc., eventually we will cover row 1012 completely. So at intermediate steps, some rows are not fully covered yet. The lower bound argument considered the final covering, not intermediate steps. The function f(i) is defined based on the final covering: after all rectangles are placed, each row i will be covered by some rectangles (maybe multiple). The final covering must cover all columns up to i-1 for each row i. So after the entire covering, f(i) = max c_R among rectangles with s_R <= i. In the final covering, we have many rectangles, each with start row s_R. Let's compute f(i) after all rectangles are placed. For the construction, we have rectangles with start rows s_1 = c_1+1 = 1013, s_2 = c_2+1 = 507, s_3 = c_3+1 = 254? Actually c_2 = floor(1012/2) = 506, s_2 = 506+1 = 507. c_3 = floor(506/2) = 253, s_3 = 254. So the start rows are decreasing: s_1 > s_2 > s_3 > ... > s_k = 2? Actually, after k steps, we get down to size 1. So the start rows are decreasing. So the function f(i) for i >= s_1 = 1013 is max of c_R for s_R <= i. Since s_R <= i includes all rectangles with start row <= i. Since s_1 = 1013, for i >= 1013, all rectangles have start row <= i, so f(i) = max of all c_R = c_1 = 1012? Actually, c_R values are decreasing: c_1 = 1012, c_2 = 506, c_3 = 253, etc. So the max is c_1 = 1012. So for i >= 1013, f(i) = 1012. For i between s_2 = 507 and s_1 - 1 = 1012, the rectangles with start row <= i are those with start rows s_2, s_3, ..., s_k. The max c_R among those is c_2 = 506. So for i in [507,1012], f(i) = 506