es rectangles R_t^L that cover rows from c_t+1 to n_{t-1} and columns 1..c_t. Let's see if the union of these rectangles indeed covers all points (i,j) with i>j.\n\nTake n = 2025. c_1 = floor(2025/2) = 1012. R_1^L covers rows 1013..2025, columns 1..1012. So it covers all points (i,j) with i >= 1013 and j <= 1012. In particular, for any i >= 1013, all j < i are covered because j <= i-1, but j may be > 1012 if i is large. For i = 1500, j can be up to 1499, but R_1^L only covers j up to 1012. So points (1500, 1200) are not covered by R_1^L. However, later rectangles may cover those points. Let's examine R_2^L: c_2 = floor(1012/2) = 506. R_2^L covers rows 507..1012, columns 1..506. So it covers points with i in [507,1012] and j <= 506. So for i = 1500, row 1500 is not covered by R_2^L because its rows are only up to 1012. So points (1500, 1200) remain uncovered after R_1 and R_2. Next rectangle R_3^L: c_3 = floor(506/2) = 253. R_3^L covers rows 254..506, columns 1..253. So still not covering row 1500. So the construction only covers rows up to n_{t-1} = the current size (starting from n_0 = 2025, then n_1 = 1012, n_2 = 506, etc.). So after k steps, the largest row covered by any rectangle is n_0 = 2025 (by R_1). The next rectangles cover rows up to n_1 = 1012, then n_2 = 506, etc. So the union of all rectangles covers rows in intervals: [c_1+1, n_0] = [1013,2025] (R_1), [c_2+1, n_1] = [507,1012] (R_2), [c_3+1, n_2] = [254,506] (R_3), etc., down to [c_k+1, n_{k-1}] = [2, something]. So the union of rows covered is the whole range [2,2025] (since the intervals are nested and cover all rows >= 2). Indeed, the intervals partition the rows: each row i belongs to exactly one rectangle: find the smallest t such that i > c_t (i.e., i >= c_t+1) and i <= n_{t-1}. Since c_t = floor(n_{t-1}/2), the intervals are [floor(n_{t-1}/2)+1, n_{t-1}]. So they partition the rows: each row i belongs to the interval where i > floor(n_{t-1}/2) and i <= n_{t-1}. So each row is covered by exactly