n holds. So condition f(i) >= i-1 is necessary: If the maximum column covered by any rectangle that starts at or before i is less than i-1, then column i-1 is not covered by any rectangle that starts at or before i, but could it be covered by a rectangle that starts after i? No, because a rectangle that starts after i (i.e., s_R > i) does not cover row i (since rows are from s_R to n). So any rectangle covering row i must have start row <= i. So the condition f(i) >= i-1 is indeed necessary. So the condition holds.\n\nNow, the bound on jumps: Suppose we have f(i-1) = c. Then we need to cover column i-1 in row i-1. Since f(i-1) = c >= (i-1)-1 = i-2, we have c >= i-2. So i <= c+2. So i is at most c+2. So the next row i where we need to consider a jump cannot be larger than c+2? Actually, we are analyzing the next rectangle start row s_R. The rectangle that increases f may start at row s_R > i-1. But we need to ensure that for all rows up to s_R - 1, the condition f(i) >= i-1 holds. Since f(i) is monotone, if f(i-1) = c, then for i = i-1 + 1 = i, we need f(i) >= i-1. But if we haven't added a new rectangle yet, f(i) = c (since no rectangle started at i). So we need c >= i-1. So i <= c+1. So the next row where we need to start a rectangle cannot be beyond c+1. Actually, we need to ensure that for each row i, f(i) >= i-1. If we have f(i-1) = c, then for row i, if we don't start a new rectangle at i, then f(i) = c (since no new rectangle started). Then we need c >= i-1. So i <= c+1. So the next row where we need to start a rectangle must be at most c+1. So the start row s_R must satisfy s_R <= c+1. But earlier we derived s_R <= c+2. Let's be precise: Suppose we have f(i-1) = c. Then for row i = i-1+1, we need f(i) >= i-1. If we don't start a new rectangle at row i, then f(i) = f(i-1) = c. So we need c >= i-1. So i <= c+1. So the next rectangle must start at row s_R <= c+1 (if we need to increase f). Actually, if c = i-2, then i <= c+2 = i, okay. Let's test with numbers: Suppose f(1) = 0 (c=0). For row 2, we need f(2) >= 1. If we don't start a rectangle at row 2, f(2) = 0 < 1, so we must start a rectangle at row 2. So s_R = 2 <= c+1 = 1? Wait, c=0, c+1 = 1, but s_R = 2 > 1. So our inequality s_R <= c+1 fails. Let's recalc: For row i, we need f(i) >= i-1. If f(i-1) = c, then we need to ensure that for row i, either we start a new rectangle at row i (so f(i) = max(c, c_R) >= i-1) or we already have c >= i-1. So if c < i-1, we need to start a rectangle at row i. So the condition for needing a new rectangle at row i is c < i-1. So the start row s_R must be such that c_{prev} < s_R - 1. So s_R can be larger than c+1 if c already satisfies c >= s_R - 1? Actually, we need to start a rectangle at row s_R when c_{prev} < s_R - 1. So s_R can be any row where the previous max column is insufficient. So the inequality s_R <= c+2 may hold. Let's derive: Suppose we have f(s_R - 1) = c. Since we haven't started a rectangle at s_R yet, we need to ensure that f(s_R - 1) >= (s_R - 1) - 1 = s_R - 2. So c >= s_R - 2. Therefore s_R <= c + 2. So the start row s_R can be at most c+2. This matches earlier derived bound. So the start row cannot be too far beyond c. So the next rectangle's start row s_R is at most c+2. So the jump from c to c_R can be at most c_R <= s_R - 1 <= (c+2) - 1 = c+1. So the jump is at most +1. So each rectangle can increase f by at most 1. So the number of rectangles needed to reach f(n) >= n-1 is at least n-1. That contradicts known construction with O(log n). Something is wrong with the definition of f(i). Let's examine more carefully: The function f(i) = max{c_R | s_R <= i} is defined for left-anchored rectangles of the form [s_R,n] x [1,c_R]. For each rectangle, c_R is the max column covered (since columns are from 1 to c_R). So f(i) is the max column covered by any rectangle that starts at or before i. However, the union of columns covered in row i may be the union of intervals [1,c_R] for all rectangles that start at or before