    "solution": "### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:** I have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is 4048.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**\n1. **Reduction to the diagonal.** Because each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares forms a permutation matrix. By permuting rows and columns we may assume the uncovered squares are precisely the main diagonal squares $(i,i)$ for $1\\le i\\le n$, where $n=2025$.\n2. **Decomposition into two independent parts.** The remaining squares split into the lower triangular region\n   $$L=\\{(i,j):i>j\\}$$\n   and the upper triangular region\n   $$U=\\{(i,j):i<j\\}.$$   Any axis\u2011aligned rectangle that avoids the diagonal must satisfy either $\\max\\{\\text{columns}\\}<\\min\\{\\text{rows}\\}$ (a *left\u2011anchored* rectangle, which can only lie in $L$) or $\\max\\{\\text{rows}\\}<\\min\\{\\text{columns}\\}$ (a *right\u2011anchored* rectangle, which can only lie in $U$). Hence the problem separates into covering $L$ with left\u2011anchored rectangles and covering $U$ with right\u2011anchored rectangles; the total number of tiles is the sum of the two numbers.\n3. **A lower bound for $L$.** For each column $j$ ($1\\le j\\le n-1$) consider the square $c_j=(j+1,j)\\in L$. Any left\u2011anchored rectangle $R=[a,b]\\times[c,d]$ that contains $c_j$ must satisfy $d\\ge j$ and $a\\le j+1\\le b$. Because $R$ is left\u2011anchored we have $d<a$, so $a> d\\ge j$; together with $a\\le j+1$ this forces $a=j+1$ and $d=j$. Consequently the rightmost column of $R$ is exactly $j$, and $R$ cannot contain any column $>j$. Thus a single rectangle can cover at most one of the critical squares $c_j$. Since the $c_j$ are all distinct, at least $n-1$ left\u2011anchored rectangles are necessary to cover $L$.\n4. **A matching construction for $L$.** For each $j$ ($1\\le j\\le n-1$) place the rectangle\n   $$R_j^{L}=\\{(i,j):j+1\\le i\\le n\\}=[j+1,n]\\times\\{j\\}.$$   This rectangle is left\u2011anchored because $\\max\\{\\text{columns}\\}=j<j+1=\\min\\{\\text{rows}\\}$. The rectangles $R_j^{L}$ are pairwise disjoint (they occupy different columns) and together they cover every square of $L$ (every pair $(i,j)$ with $i>j$ belongs to $R_j^{L}$). Hence $L$ can be tiled with exactly $n-1$ left\u2011anchored rectangles, meeting the lower bound.\n5. **Symmetry for $U$.** By reflecting the board across the main diagonal, the same argument applies to the upper triangular region. For each $i$ ($1\\le i\\le n-1$) define the right\u2011anchored rectangle\n   $$R_i^{U}=\\{(i,j):i+1\\le j\\le n\\}=[i]\\times[i+1,n].$$   These $n-1$ rectangles are disjoint, avoid the diagonal, and together cover all squares of $U$. Thus $U$ also requires exactly $n-1$ rectangles.\n6. **Conclusion.** The whole off\u2011diagonal set $L\\cup U$ can be tiled with\n   $$(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2$$\n   rectangles, and the lower\u2011bound argument shows that fewer rectangles are impossible. For $n=2025$ we obtain\n   $$2\\cdot2025-2=4048.$$   Therefore the minimum number of tiles Matilda must place is **4048**.\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet $n=2025$ and consider the $n\\times n$ board of unit squares.  A *tile* is a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; equivalently a tile is a set $I\\times J$ where $I,J\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots,n\\}$ are intervals of consecutive integers.\n\n#### 1.  Reduction to the diagonal\n\nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered square, the uncovered squares form a permutation matrix: there exists a permutation $\\sigma$ of $\\{1,\\dots,n\\}$ such that the uncovered squares are\n$$\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}.$$\nPermuting the rows by $i\\mapsto i$ and the columns by $j\\mapsto\\sigma^{-1}(j)$ is a bijection of the board that sends the uncovered squares to the main diagonal $D=\\{(i,i):1\\le i\\le n\\}$.  This operation does not change the existence or the number of tiles, because it merely relabels rows and columns.  Hence we may assume from now on that the uncovered squares are exactly the diagonal squares.\n\n#### 2.  Geometry of admissible rectangles\n\nLet $R=I\\times J$ be a rectangle that does **not** contain any diagonal square.  Write $I=[a,b]$ and $J=[c,d]$ with $a\\le b$, $c\\le d$.  For any $i\\in I$ we must have $i\\notin J$.  Consequently either $d<i$ for all $i\\in I$ or $c>i$ for all $i\\in I$.  In the first case $J\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots,a-1\\}$; we call $R$ **left\u2011anchored**.  In the second case $J\\subseteq\\{b+1,\\dots,n\\}$; we call $R$ **right\u2011anchored**.  Thus every admissible rectangle is of one of the two forms\n\\[\n\\text{left\u2011anchored: }[s,b]\\times[1,c]\\quad(c<s),\\qquad\\text{right\u2011anchored: }[1,s]\\times[d,n]\\quad(s<d).\n\\]\nThe lower triangular region\n$$L=\\{(i,j):i>j\\}$$\ncan be covered only by left\u2011anchored rectangles, and the upper triangular region\n$$U=\\{(i,j):i<j\\}$$\nonly by right\u2011anchored rectangles.\n\n#### 3.  Lower bound for the lower triangle\n\nFor each column $j\\in\\{1,\\dots,n-1\\}$ consider the *critical square* $c_j=(j+1,j)\\in L$.  Let $R=[a,b]\\times[c,d]$ be a left\u2011anchored rectangle that contains $c_j$.  Since $c_j\\in R$ we have $a\\le j+1\\le b$ and $c\\le j\\le d$.  Because $R$ is left\u2011anchored we have $d<a$.  Combining $d\\ge j$ with $a\\le j+1$ yields $j< a\\le j+1$, hence $a=j+1$ and $d=j$.  Therefore the rightmost column of $R$ is exactly $j$, and $R$ cannot contain any column larger than $j$.\n\nConsequently a single left\u2011anchored rectangle can cover **at most one** critical square $c_j$.  Since the $n-1$ critical squares are distinct, any family of left\u2011anchored rectangles that covers $L$ must contain at least $n-1$ rectangles.\n\n#### 4.  A covering attaining the bound\n\nFor each $j\\in\\{1,\\dots,n-1\\}$ define the rectangle\n\\[\nR_j^{L}=\\{(i,j):\\;j+1\\le i\\le n\\}=[j+1,n]\\times\\{j\\}.\n\\]\nBecause $\\max\\{\\text{columns}\\}=j<j+1=\\min\\{\\text{rows}\\}$, $R_j^{L}$ is left\u2011anchored and contains no diagonal square.  The rectangles $R_1^{L},R_2^{L},\\dots,R_{n-1}^{L}$ are pairwise disjoint (they occupy different columns) and their union is exactly $L$ (every pair $(i,j)$ with $i>j$ belongs to $R_j^{L}$).  Hence $L$ can be tiled with exactly $n-1$ left\u2011anchored rectangles, matching the lower bound.\n\n#### 5.  The upper triangle\n\nReflecting the board across the main diagonal exchanges $L$ and $U$ and swaps left\u2011anchored with right\u2011anchored rectangles.  Therefore the same argument shows that $U$ requires at least $n-1$ right\u2011anchored rectangles and can be tiled with exactly $n-1$ of them.  An explicit tiling is obtained by, for each $i\\in\\{1,\\dots,n-1\\}$,\n\\[\nR_i^{U}=\\{(i,j):\\;i+1\\le j\\le n\\}=\\{i\\}\\times[i+1,n].\n\\]\nThese rectangles are right\u2011anchored, disjoint, and cover $U$.\n\n#### 6.  Minimal number of tiles for the whole board\n\nAny tiling of the whole board must contain at least $(n-1)$ left\u2011anchored rectangles (to cover $L$) and at least $(n-1)$ right\u2011anchored rectangles (to cover $U$).  Thus at least $2n-2$ rectangles are necessary.  The constructions of Sections\u202f4 and\u202f5 give a tiling with exactly $2n-2$ rectangles, so this bound is tight.\n\nFor $n=2025$ we obtain\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048.\n\\]\nTherefore the minimum number of rectangular tiles Matilda must place is **4048**.\n\n### Final Answer ###\nThe minimum number of tiles required is 4048.",
    "final_answer": 4048
}
